-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/49ed6e36-3e96-55c8-b4a5-b2fb6959af00
-- title:
--   Drinfeld quotient model of the descended supersingular chart, q=3
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime, pinned by the hypothesis `hq3` to $q=3$; $M'$ is a nonzero level with $q \nmid M'$ (`hqM'`); and $\ell_g$ is a prime with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$ (`hℓg`, `hℓg12`, `hℓgM'`). The level group $H_1 \le (\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ is fixed by `hH₁` to be the intersection of `levelH q M'`, the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell_g)^\times$.
--
--   Arithmetic base. $A$ is a valuation subring of an algebraic closure $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ with $q$ a non-unit of $A$ (`hA`, i.e. `A.LiesOverPrime q`); write $\kappa$ for its residue field. $W$ is a finite set of places of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,\kappa\,M'$ over $\kappa$ — this field being the $\kappa$-subfield of $\kappa$-Laurent series generated by the $q$-expansions `jqModC` and `jqNModC` of $j$ and of $j(M'\tau)$, and a place being a valuation subring containing $\kappa$, distinct from the whole field, whose valuation ring is a principal ideal ring — and `hW` says that $W$ consists exactly of the supersingular places `ssPlaces q M' κ`, i.e. the rational affine geometric places whose value at `jGeomGen` is a supersingular $j$-invariant in the sense of `ssJSet q κ` (every elliptic curve with that invariant has no nonzero $q$-torsion point). An element $s \in W$ is fixed. The hypothesis `hle` asserts the inclusion $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M' \le \mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-subfields of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-Laurent series, the first generated by the images of the series $\mathrm{qExpand}\,d\,(j)$ for $d \mid M'$, the second by the image of the modular function field of level $\Gamma_{\mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M'}(q^2M')$. $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ from $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,\kappa\,M'$: a valuation subring `R₀.integers` whose intersection with $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is $A$, a surjective residue homomorphism onto the target with kernel the maximal ideal and compatible with $A \to \kappa$ on constants, a scaling axiom, and a degree- and divisor-compatible map on places. The hypothesis `hR₀` says that every Laurent series with coefficients in $A$ whose image lies in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ is $R_0$-integral, with $R_0$-residue equal, as a $\kappa$-Laurent series, to the coefficientwise residue.
--
--   Constants. $k_0$ is an intermediate field $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq k_0 \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and $\pi_0 \in k_0$ lies in $A$ (`hπ`); write $A_0$ for the contraction $A \cap k_0$. The hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres`, `hκ` require that $A_0$ be a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal $(\pi_0)$, henselian, with algebraically closed residue field, and that every element of $A$ be congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element of $k_0 \cap A$. Further: $\ell$ is a prime with $3 \le \ell$, $\ell \ne q$, $\ell \nmid M'$ (`hℓ`, `hℓ3`, `hℓq`, `hℓM'`); $\zeta_0 \in k_0$ is a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity (`hζ₀`); $\xi \in k_0$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity (`hξ`); $\varpi_t \in k_0$ lies in $A$ and satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\,u$ with $u$ a unit of $A$ (`hϖtA`, `hϖt`); $K_b$ is an intermediate field of $k_0$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $K_b = \bot$ (`hKb`), $A_b$ a valuation subring of $K_b$ which is the contraction of $A$ (`hAb`), with maximal ideal generated by a nonzero $\varpi_b$ (`hϖb`, `hϖb0`); `htame` requires $\ell_g - 1$ to be a unit of $A_0$; $\varpi_t' \in A_0$ equals $\varpi_t$ (`hϖt'`), and $m \ge 1$ satisfies $\pi_0^m = \varpi_t' \cdot (\text{unit})$ (`hm1`, `hm`).
--
--   The rigid level field and its supersingular chart. $K_\ell$ is an intermediate field of $k_0$ in the $k_0$-Laurent series field, equal by `hKℓ` to the $k_0$-subfield generated by the image of the modular function field of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$, and carrying an $A_0$-algebra structure compatible with $k_0$; $j_\ell \in K_\ell$ is nonzero and equal to the $q$-expansion of $j$ (`hjℓ`). The chart algebra is $\mathrm{chartAlgFin}\,A_0\,K_\ell\,j_\ell$, the subalgebra of elements of $K_\ell$ integral over $A_0[j_\ell]$; $y$ is a maximal ideal of it (`hy`) containing the image of $\pi_0$ (`hϖy`). For $\delta \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ and $\tau : K_\ell \simeq_{k_0} K_\ell$, the predicate $\mathrm{IsLevelAutAt}\,k_0\,q\,\xi\,q\,(q^2M')\,H_1\,\delta\,K_\ell\,\tau$ says that $\tau$ realises the $\delta$-action on modular functions of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$: for all weights $k$, all modular forms $f,g$ of that level with integral $q$-expansions and $g$ of nonzero expansion, all $x \in K_\ell$ whose Laurent expansion is $f/g$, and all embeddings $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\xi) = e^{2\pi i/q}$, the coefficientwise image of $\tau x$ under $\iota$, multiplied by the $q$-expansion of $g|_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\delta$, equals the $q$-expansion of $f|_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\delta$.
--
--   The point $y$ is constrained by: `hss`, that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism from the chart algebra to $\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the image of $j_\ell$ lies in $\mathrm{ssJSet}\,q\,\Omega$; `hover`, which identifies $y$ with the place $s$: for every $g$ in the full modular function field of level $M'$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ whose base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is $R_0$-integral and has nonnegative order at every place at which $j$ has nonnegative order, and whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, and for every $c \in k_0$ lying in $A$ whose residue in $\kappa$ is the value at $s$ of that $R_0$-residue, the difference between the base change of $g$ to $k_0$ (assumed to lie in $K_\ell$ and in the chart algebra) and $c$ lies in $y$; and `hOrbit`, which states that any maximal ideal $y'$ of the chart algebra containing $\pi_0$ and satisfying the condition just described is linked to $y$ by a level automorphism: there are $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ with $\mathrm{IsLevelAutAt}\,\dots\,\gamma^{-1}\,K_\ell\,\tau$ preserving the chart algebra and satisfying $f \in y \leftrightarrow \tau f \in y'$ for $f$ in the chart. Also fixed are $j$-data at level $q$: `hjK` places $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,k_0\,q$ in $K_\ell$, `hjC` places it in the chart algebra, and `ha₀y` says its difference from the constant $a_0 \in A_0$ lies in $y$.
--
--   The rigid chart hypotheses. $B_t$ is an $A_0$-subalgebra of $K_\ell$ and $W_t$ a valuation subring of $K_\ell$ with $B_t \subseteq W_t$ (`hBW`). The hypothesis `hS1` (fourteen clauses, summarised here) requires: the chart algebra lies in $B_t$; $K_\ell$ is the fraction field of $B_t$; $B_t$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A_0$; $B_t/\pi_0B_t$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; $W_t \cap k_0 = A_0$; the maximal ideal of $W_t$ is generated by $\pi_0$ and $W_t$ is a discrete valuation ring; $y$ is the contraction of that maximal ideal to the chart algebra; $W_t$ is the localisation of $B_t$ at it; for every $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field of $A_0$ there is a surjective ring homomorphism $\rho$ from $B_t$ onto $\mathrm{CoordRing}\,q$ of that residue field (the quotient of $\kappa_0[x_0,x_1]$ by $\mathrm{drinfeldPoly} - 1$) with kernel the contraction of the maximal ideal of $W_t$, compatible with constants via the residue map of $A_0$, and equivariant for level automorphisms attached to $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ through an element $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma, c)$ of $\mathrm{hSubgroup}\,q$ acting by `hAction`, with $c \ne 1$ for nontrivial $\tau$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$, together with a companion level automorphism realising the same $c$ with trivial $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/q)$-part; $B_t$ is stable under level automorphisms for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$; every prime of $B_t$ containing $\pi_0$ is obtained from the maximal ideal of $W_t$ by such an automorphism; and an element of $B_t$ lying in all translates of that maximal ideal is divisible by $\pi_0$.
--
--   The integer $n$ satisfies $1 \le n$, $n \mid q+1$ (`hn1`, `hnq`) and, by `hn`, $n = 2\,\mathrm{placeWidthChar}\,q\,M'\,s$, the width character of $s$ (for $q=3$ the value $6$ or $1$ according as the $j$-value vanishes or not, divided by the $j$-ramification of $s$). Further data: $\gamma_0 \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ (`hγ₀Γ`, `hγ₀Γ0`), a level automorphism $\tau_0$ at $\gamma_0^{-1}$ (`hτ₀`) preserving $W_t$ (`hτ₀W`); an element $\zeta_c$ of the $\pi_0$-adic completion of $A_0$ with $\zeta_c^n = 1$ and $\zeta_c^k - 1$ a unit for $0 < k < n$ (`hζc`, `hζcprim`); and a finite set `ends` of subrings of $K_\ell$. The hypothesis `hR7` (twelve clauses, summarised here) requires: $\tau_0$ acts on $B_t$ with exact order $n$ and its powers exhaust the level automorphisms attached to $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$; `ends` has $q+1$ elements, is permuted transitively by the level automorphisms for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$, each end is stable under $\tau_0$, and distinct ends are separated by an element which is a non-unit in one and a unit in the other; there is a finite-type $A_0$-subalgebra $B_c$, stable under the level automorphisms for $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$, contained in every end and with every end a localisation of it; a level automorphism carrying an end into $W_t$ preserves $W_t$; for each end $O$: $O \subseteq W_t$, $O$ is local and noetherian, contains the chart algebra, satisfies $O \cap k_0 = A_0$, every element of $O$ differs from a constant by a non-unit, and there is a crossing-model description, namely $\pi_0 \in O$, elements $c_x, c_y$ with $c_xc_y = \pi_0^m \cdot (\text{unit})$, and an isomorphism $\iota$ of the completion of $O$ with $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}$ over the $\pi_0$-adic completion of $A_0$ with parameter $\pi_0^m$ (the quotient of a two-variable power series ring by $X_0X_1 - \pi_0^m$) carrying constants to constants, $c_x$ and $c_y$ to unit multiples of $U$ and $V$ with $c_y$ in and $c_x$ outside the maximal ideal of $W_t$, carrying $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,k_0\,q - a_0$ to a unit multiple of $V^e$ with $e \ge 1$, and conjugating $\tau_0$ into an automorphism $\theta_0$ with $\theta_0(U) \equiv \zeta_cU$ and $\theta_0(V) \equiv \zeta_c^{-1}V$ modulo the square of the maximal ideal, together with the existence of a valuation subring $W_x$ (dominating $O$, a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal $(\pi_0)$, meeting $k_0$ in $A_0$, detecting $y$ on the chart algebra, and singling out $O$ among the ends) and of a finite-type, level-stable subalgebra $B_x \subseteq B_t \cap O$ with poles only where $j_\ell$ has poles, with $O$ its localisation and with a separating element; and finally a classification of the valuation subrings $V$ with $V \cap k_0 = A_0$ containing the chart algebra and with $y$ in the maximal ideal: either $B_t \subseteq V$ or $V$ dominates one of the ends. The hypotheses `hR9` and `hR10` are separation statements: `hR9` provides an element of $B_t$ avoiding every valuation subring which meets $k_0$ in $A_0$, has $\pi_0$ in its maximal ideal and is non-integral over $j_\ell$ in the stated sense, and `hR10` provides, for every maximal ideal $y''$ of the chart algebra containing $\pi_0$ which is not in the level-automorphism orbit of $y$, an element of $B_t$ avoiding the corresponding valuation subrings.
--
--   Descent data. $G$ is the subgroup of $\mathrm{Aut}(K_\ell/k_0)$ generated by the level automorphisms attached to $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ (`hG`), $K_0 = K_\ell^G$ is its fixed field (`hK₀`), $B_0$ is the $A_0$-subalgebra consisting of the $G$-invariant elements of $B_t$ (`hB₀`), and $W_0'$ is the valuation subring of $K_0$ obtained by intersecting $W_t$ with $K_0$ (`hW₀'`).
--
--   Conclusion. For every $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field $\kappa$ of $A$, and under the assumption that $\mathrm{CoordRing}\,q\,\kappa$ is a domain, there exist a subgroup $C_s$ of the group of $(q+1)$-st roots of unity of $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$ and a ring homomorphism $\rho : B_0 \to \mathrm{quotField}\,q\,\kappa\,C_s$, the latter being the subfield of the Drinfeld function field $\mathrm{Frac}(\mathrm{CoordRing}\,q\,\kappa)$ fixed by the subgroup generated by the automorphisms $\mathrm{hFunctionFieldAction}$ of the elements $(1,\zeta) \in \mathrm{hSubgroup}\,q$ for $\zeta \in C_s$, such that:
--
--   (i) $\#C_s = 2\,\mathrm{placeWidthChar}\,q\,M'\,s$;
--
--   (ii) the kernel of $\rho$ is the ideal of $B_0$ generated by the image of $\pi_0$;
--
--   (iii) for every $a \in A_0$, $\rho$ of the image of $a$ in $B_0$ equals the image, under the structure map $\kappa \to \mathrm{quotField}\,q\,\kappa\,C_s$, of the residue in $\kappa$ of $a$ viewed as an element of $A$;
--
--   (iv) every element $z$ of $\mathrm{quotField}\,q\,\kappa\,C_s$ is a quotient of values of $\rho$: there are $g, h \in B_0$ with $\rho h \ne 0$ and $z \cdot \rho h = \rho g$;
--
--   (v) an element $z$ of $\mathrm{quotField}\,q\,\kappa\,C_s$ lies in the range of $\rho$ if and only if $z$, regarded in the Drinfeld function field, lies in the image of $\mathrm{CoordRing}\,q\,\kappa$;
--
--   (vi) $\rho$ is equivariant for the level action: for every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, every $\tau : K_\ell \simeq_{k_0} K_\ell$ with $\mathrm{IsLevelAutAt}\,k_0\,q\,\xi\,q\,(q^2M')\,H_1\,\gamma^{-1}\,K_\ell\,\tau$, every witness that $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma, 1)$ lies in $\mathrm{hSubgroup}\,q$, and every $f \in B_0$ with $\tau f \in B_0$, the image of $\rho(\tau f)$ in the Drinfeld function field equals $\mathrm{hFunctionFieldAction}$ of that element applied to the image of $\rho f$.
--
--   This is the identification of the special fibre of the descended supersingular affine chart on the modular curve of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ — in the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$ diamond frame and in the small-prime case $q=3$ — with an affine piece of a quotient of the Drinfeld (Deligne–Lusztig) curve over the residue field $\kappa$, together with the compatibility of this identification with the level action and with constants. It is used by the statements establishing that the invariant chart algebra is integrally closed and that its localisations at supersingular points are discrete valuation rings.
